WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.591
- Hi, everyone, welcome
to this afternoon's

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:12.660
Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities event,

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.140
our distinguished speaker series

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.290
and we have a very exciting speaker today.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:22.570
We have the mathematician
and author Jordan Ellenberg

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.930
and his talk title is

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:29.930
"I prove a theorem and the house
expands, geometry and poetry."

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.770
But before I introduce our
speaker for this afternoon,

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.460
I would first like to thank
Richard and Barbara Franke

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.016
for their generosity

00:00:40.016 --> 00:00:44.030
in funding and supporting
the Franke program

00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:49.030
and many other activities that
cross disciplinary boundaries

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.780
and give opportunities
for scholars young and old

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.570
to dare to try research projects

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:01.700
that are not confined
to single disciplines.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:05.370
And I also want to remind
all of you assembled

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.350
that we are recording this event

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.230
and therefore all participants
must mute their videos.

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:16.230
And as is customary for our
events if you have a question,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.610
please go ahead and put it in the chat.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:24.490
And our, we tend to have
a Q&amp;A offer of a talk

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:29.250
unless Dr. Camp and I are
keeping track of the chat.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.320
So if it's a burning question,

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.020
we'll go ahead and ask it right away,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.680
if not, we will bank
it for the Q&amp;A session.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:41.530
So it is my absolute
delight today to introduce

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.190
our wonderful speaker Jordan.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:48.040
Professor Jordan Ellenberg
grew up in Potomac in Maryland,

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.540
and it turns out that
math might be in his genes

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.830
because his parents
are both statisticians.

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:57.480
He excelled in mathematics
from a very young age

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.560
and competed for the U.S.
representing the United States

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.920
in the International Mathematics
Olympiad three times,

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.010
winning two gold medals and a silver.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:09.910
This I should point out is

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:14.910
absolutely no mean achievement
to get two golds and silver.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.860
That is super impressive Jordan.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.029
He went to college at Harvard,

00:02:20.029 --> 00:02:24.620
sadly you know, the other
place over there and attained

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.530
a master's degree in fiction
writing from Johns Hopkins.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:29.770
And then he returned

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.130
back to Harvard for a PhD in mathematics.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.230
After graduate school,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.990
he did a brief stint as
a postdoc at Princeton

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:39.980
and in 2004, he joined

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.740
the faculty of the University
of Wisconsin at Madison,

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.580
where he's now

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.340
the John D MacArthur
professor of mathematics,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.500
His research centers on
fields of number theory

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.930
and algebra geometry you know,
the portions of mathematics

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:56.168
that address very fundamental questions

00:02:56.168 --> 00:02:58.950
about the nature of equations

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.233
and their solutions in whole numbers.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.180
Jordan's research

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.770
has uncovered new and
unexpected connections

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.390
between these subjects
and algebraic topology,

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:11.800
the study of abstract
higher dimensional shapes

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.200
and the relationships between them.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.930
Ellenberg was a plenary speaker

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:18.600
at the 2013 joint mathematics meeting

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.420
one of the largest mathematics
conferences in the world.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:23.510
And he has lectured about his research

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:27.740
in multiple countries and multiple venues.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.890
He has a ton of awards and honors

00:03:30.890 --> 00:03:33.390
and recognition because I'm
dying to hear him speak,

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:36.100
I'm gonna give you a
very abbreviated version.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:38.060
He was awarded an NSF career grant

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.204
and Alfred P.Sloan Foundation Fellowship,

00:03:40.204 --> 00:03:42.350
he was a fellow is a fellow

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:44.840
of the American Mathematical Society

00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:45.673
and a Guggenheim fellow
and Assignments fellow.

00:03:45.673 --> 00:03:48.486
So, Jordan has been writing
for a general audience

00:03:48.486 --> 00:03:53.486
about mathematics for more than 15 years

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:57.870
and his work has appeared in many of the

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.420
most industrious outlets
in New York times,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.640
Wall Street Journal, Washington
Post Wire, Boston globe

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.683
and he is the author of Do
The Math column in Slate.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:13.720
His novel Grasshopper King
was a finalist for the 2004

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:17.110
New York Public Library
Young Lions Fiction Award.

00:04:17.110 --> 00:04:19.840
His 2014 book, so this is not

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.950
today he will be talking to
us about his latest book,

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.020
but as you see, it's not his first book.

00:04:26.020 --> 00:04:28.310
He's had many wonderful books before,

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.210
and I was first introduced to his writing

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:35.440
when I read his 2014
book, How Not To Be Wrong

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.410
and that was a New York Times bestseller

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.923
and a Sunday Times London bestseller.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.420
He lives in Madison, Wisconsin,

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.500
and he's speaking to
us from his home today.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.700
And in particular,

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.950
I just wanna point out if
you haven't already bought it

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:57.520
Jordan's latest book is called SHAPE,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.030
the hidden geometry of
information, biology, strategy,

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.990
democracy, and just about everything else.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:05.140
It's actually there in the title

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.800
everything else is in the title.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.800
So Jordan, without further ado

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.390
I am so delighted you're
here with us today

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:13.490
I wish it could have been in person,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:16.800
but we can't wait to hear from you.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.630
Thank you so much for coming.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:19.930
- Well, thank you so much Priya

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.420
and thanks to the Franke's
for making this talk possible.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:27.670
I wrote a book about geometry
and this is a picture

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.090
this is the front of
piece of the book actually

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:31.060
it's sort of a map of the whole book

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.066
and if I were giving
a talk about the book,

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:34.380
I would just have to
cover all of these topics

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.099
in 45 minutes, which would
be pretty hard to do.

00:05:37.099 --> 00:05:42.030
But maybe let me sort of zero
in on one particular aspect,

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:43.560
which is that if you look sort of

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:48.560
very near the center of the
map, we see a box called poetry.

00:05:49.830 --> 00:05:51.350
And I did not expect

00:05:51.350 --> 00:05:53.530
that when I was writing
a book about geometry,

00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:57.560
I was gonna find myself talking
about poetry so very much

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.258
and in so many different contexts.

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:02.260
And so I thought for this
audience and for this center,

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:05.020
it seemed the perfect time to
sort of spend a little time

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:08.970
just talking about where
those connections come from

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.810
and what they seem to
mean to the geometers

00:06:11.810 --> 00:06:13.360
and the poets who participated in them.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.840
And I guess if you guys have questions

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.440
about any of the other things on this map

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.720
and what they have to do
with poetry or geometry

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.290
feel free to pipe in some of them are,

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:22.123
some of these are names

00:06:22.123 --> 00:06:23.820
of people who are gonna
show up in this talk

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.436
and others are not,

00:06:26.436 --> 00:06:29.750
'cause we only have a limited
amount of time together.

00:06:29.750 --> 00:06:32.920
Okay so, if you think
about geometry and poetry

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:37.860
probably there's like one number one line

00:06:39.108 --> 00:06:41.960
that you have heard
and it's a line I think

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:44.220
we don't know the source of this line,

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:47.520
like know the line "Euclid
alone has looked on beauty bare"

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.690
and it's from a sonnet by Edna St. Vincent Millay,

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.663
who you see in this picture here.

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.090
But this is not really
what I'm gonna talk about.

00:06:55.090 --> 00:06:55.923
In some sense,

00:06:55.923 --> 00:06:56.756
I wanna sort of set this
up as a little bit of

00:06:56.756 --> 00:07:00.253
something I wanna gesture
the antithesis to.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:05.170
Because malaise Euclid presented here

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:08.760
is this kind of singular on earthly figure

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.330
who kind of gets blasted
with enlightenment

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.717
by a shaft of insight on what Millay calls

00:07:13.717 --> 00:07:16.030
"a holy terrible day."

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.420
And in this poem, it's kind of like

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:19.760
there's Euclid, he's alone, he's Euclid

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.230
the rest of us or the rest of us.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:22.240
Like maybe if we're lucky,

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:23.850
we kind of get to hear beauty's footsteps

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.799
wandering way down a hallway
or something like that.

00:07:26.799 --> 00:07:31.680
And that is not really
geometry as I see it

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.613
and I think it's not really
true to the history of geometry

00:07:33.613 --> 00:07:35.010
that I wanna talk about today.

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:36.100
I mean, look, don't get me wrong

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:38.070
I am a mathematician, that's my job

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.680
I get a lot of benefit from geometry

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:45.520
this kind of mystique that
it has for a lot of people.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.050
It feels good when people
think that the work you do

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:50.660
must be like mysterious, and eternal,

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:52.450
and elevated above the common plane.

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:53.740
Like people, like how
was your day at work?

00:07:53.740 --> 00:07:55.320
And you're like, oh, you
know boring and terrible

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.550
the usual stuff like that, okay.

00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.280
So that benefits us to an extent,

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.780
but it's not really true to our practice

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.140
or the way we see what we do.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:05.620
So we're just gonna kind
of take a tour through

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.680
some other views and how
they relate to this one.

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:14.680
So let me just start, with
this very famous dinner party

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:19.943
that takes place in the 19th century,

00:08:19.943 --> 00:08:22.992
and two of the guests
are Wordsworth and Keats

00:08:22.992 --> 00:08:24.400
who you see here.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.370
And Keats at this party is
kind of trolling Wordsworth

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.440
he's by insulting Isaac Newton,

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:32.210
who was a favorite of Wordsworth saying,

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.696
Newton had destroyed all
the poetry of the rainbow

00:08:35.696 --> 00:08:39.290
by reducing it to prismatic
colors and then he'd like

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:41.800
even this famous toast
confusion to mathematics

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:43.110
and this sort of party is divided

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.300
between the people who wanna
toast confused mathematics,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.000
the people who don't feel
comfortable with that,

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:48.793
like Wordsworth.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.450
Later, maybe I'll even read
a little bit of Keats here

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:52.360
because he later writes

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.550
a philosophy will clip an Angel's wings,

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:58.230
conquer mysteries by rule
in line there's the geometry

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:02.060
empty the haunted air and no
mud mine, unweave a rainbow.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.300
So unweaving the rainbow

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.420
as you see here depicted by Pink Floyd

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.980
on their cover of Dark Side
of the Moon that's almost,

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.460
that's sort of since then
has become like a metaphor

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:15.460
for the mean thing that geometry
does to beauty to nature.

00:09:16.287 --> 00:09:19.810
It dissolves it, it breaks
it down to something

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:24.470
that is much less interesting,
namely mathematics.

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.788
Okay, this was not Wordsworth view.

00:09:28.788 --> 00:09:32.460
Wordsworth by contrast

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.810
saw Newton as a kind
of transcendent figure

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.290
exactly the kind that
romantic poetry should admire.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:40.100
He famously called Newton,

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.600
I guess it's not like a 100%
who's he's referring to,

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:44.269
but it is steady or frightening

00:09:44.269 --> 00:09:47.880
a mind forever voyaging
through strange seas

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.750
of thought alone.

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.090
That's a little bit like
notice that alone, right?

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.690
That echoes what Millay
said, kind of the same vibe.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.130
Similarly, that's from the preludes

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:01.440
and let me give you something
else from the preludes.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.700
Oh, I don't know why I have a
random cursor stuck in there

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:05.990
sorry. that's a screenshot thing.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.730
That the cursor is not
equally it's not Wordsworth,

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:09.640
but the words are

00:10:12.007 --> 00:10:14.670
"Mighty is the charm
of those abstractions."

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:17.410
Those abstractions means that
the abstractions of geometry

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:21.600
to a mind beset with images
and haunted by itself.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.340
You know, I never noticed
until this second,

00:10:24.340 --> 00:10:25.800
because I don't usually
use that Keats quote.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.460
That Keats says, "Empty the haunted air."

00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:30.570
So words within Keats

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:34.900
are both, (indistinct) geometry
as an antidote to haunting

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.220
I should make a note for myself

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.250
'cause I haven't seen
that connection before.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:40.599
But for Keats that's good,

00:10:40.599 --> 00:10:43.770
'cause haunting is like the
presence of like true spirits

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.753
that are interesting and math is boring.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:49.930
Wordsworth thinks it's good

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.437
to be charmed and to kind
of heal your hauntedness.

00:10:54.437 --> 00:10:56.150
But he's ready, this is
actually kind of crazy,

00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:00.210
he's writing, this is part
of him telling a story

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:02.970
it's kind of a crazy story of

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.610
a guy who's shipwreck on a desert island

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:09.950
and he has nothing with
him, but Euclid's element.

00:11:09.950 --> 00:11:11.460
This kind of famous book of Euclid's

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.240
that lays out the basics of geometry

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:16.200
and he's like completely
depressed, and alone and hungry

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:21.200
and in Wordsworth's poem,
he kind of suits himself.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:23.420
This is the moment at which he like

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:26.630
his constellation is
the presence of Euclid,

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:31.210
the presence of geometry
enables him to kind of

00:11:31.210 --> 00:11:34.823
get his mind away from
like all his regrets.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:39.070
I know that's kind of a weird story

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:41.120
and you're like, why would,

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:44.360
that seems like a little
bit of a rickety story

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:47.300
for Wordsworth to make up
and put in his preludes

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:48.133
and one thing that's interesting about it

00:11:48.133 --> 00:11:50.190
is that it's basically true.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.700
He took it from the memoirs
of a different Newton,

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:54.870
John Newton who, I mean,

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.790
it's a whole story but
he really was shipwrecked

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:58.540
he really did have Euclid with him.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:02.350
And he's not so famous for this
he's famous because he then

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:04.070
goes back to England and gets religion

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:07.190
and he is the author of
the hymn Amazing Grace.

00:12:07.190 --> 00:12:09.900
So interesting story.

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:13.940
But so this is Wordsworth's view,

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.400
a much more positive view
of what geometry can do

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:18.408
and the way he felt about geometry,

00:12:18.408 --> 00:12:21.140
he also felt about geometers.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.670
So one interesting moment is that

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:26.810
he becomes very good friends
with William Rowan Hamilton,

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:28.280
I don't know if that's a name
that everybody here knows,

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.570
but Hamilton is an
absolutely central figure

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:34.003
in the development of 19th
century, algebra and geometry,

00:12:34.003 --> 00:12:35.490
sort of a child prodigy.

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:38.770
And when they meet it's this kind of

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.860
very classic May, December
intellectual romance

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:44.890
where Hamilton is I think 22 or 23,

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:47.066
but he's already a professor
of astronomy of mathematics

00:12:47.066 --> 00:12:49.410
in Dublin.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:50.760
And the two of them absolutely

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.090
can not get enough of each other

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:54.740
they like go on long walks
and talk about everything.

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:59.920
Part of that, is because Hamilton himself

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.091
while being famous
today as a mathematician

00:13:03.091 --> 00:13:06.810
also wanted to be a poet, he
really wanted to be a poet

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:08.900
and wrote poetry his whole life.

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:10.913
He was always very ambivalent about it,

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.740
feeling like did he really deserve it.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:20.740
So at one point he renounces poetry

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.240
and of course he renounced
his poetry by writing a poem

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:26.610
about his pronunciation of
poetry called To Poetry.

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.780
And there's kind of a cycle
he renounced his poetry,

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:29.830
then he's like, I can't do it

00:13:29.830 --> 00:13:30.950
I gotta go back writing poems.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:34.040
Then he, he actually
renounced poetry again

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.630
and wrote a second poem called To Poetry,

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:38.180
which I haven't reproduced here

00:13:40.332 --> 00:13:42.150
and he's constantly sending
all this stuff to Wordsworth.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.070
So after he sends his
second renunciation poem

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:49.070
to Wordsworth, he really
wants Wordsworth's approval

00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:54.140
and Wordsworth, he's really nice about it.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:57.470
He, it's sort of one of the great, gentle,

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:01.830
kind let downs in early history.

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:05.800
Wordsworth writes a long
letter to Hamilton saying like,

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:08.580
I really like your poems,
they're really good

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:10.300
I wish I could encourage
you to keep writing them

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.470
but the loss to science
would just be too great.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:15.850
I couldn't allow myself to be responsible

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:18.510
for the geometry and the
mathematics and the astronomy

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.631
that wouldn't happen if I encouraged you

00:14:20.631 --> 00:14:22.910
to keep writing poems.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.330
So that's how Wordsworth
kind of handled this

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:31.330
rather delicate social
situation with his young friend.

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.693
So where are we?

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.220
I guess what we've seen

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:42.060
is that what poets
traditionally think geometry is

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:46.540
at least poets of Wordsworth Spain

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:47.700
or Millay for that matter,

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:49.170
poets were sympathetic to jump at all.

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.910
They don't think it's just
some completely boring BS,

00:14:51.910 --> 00:14:54.290
that's meant to sort of drain
the world of its spirit,

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:55.420
which is what keeps thought.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.740
What they think is that
it's some means of content

00:14:57.740 --> 00:14:58.640
with a transcendent,

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:00.940
some means of contact
with something that is

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:03.520
the same kind of thing
that romantic poetry wanted

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:05.830
to be in con, you could
call it the divine,

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:06.663
you could call it the transcendent,

00:15:06.663 --> 00:15:08.710
something beyond ordinary
human experience.

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:13.577
So what does geometers
typically think poetry is?

00:15:13.577 --> 00:15:15.103
We should look at the other side of it.

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:17.680
That's a little complicated.

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:18.920
We talked about Hamilton,

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:22.383
who I think has a pretty
mainstream relationship with Cody.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:25.930
But I also wanna talk about
James Joseph Sylvester,

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.100
who we see here.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.610
This is another

00:15:29.610 --> 00:15:33.853
big time 19th century
British mathematician.

00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:39.423
Famous by the way,

00:15:39.423 --> 00:15:41.350
to everyone who knew him
for having a gigantic head

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:42.940
that's not just the picture like that's,

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.260
he was a guy who was well-known
