00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:11.840
Okay, but really I
mean, it's a good story,

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:15.160
but really I wanna talk
about Sylvester and poetry.

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:17.898
He said at the beginning
of a speech one time,

00:18:17.898 --> 00:18:22.290
an eloquent mathematician
must from the nature of beings

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:26.053
ever remain as rare
phenomenon as a talking fish.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:28.133
It's a great line,

00:18:28.133 --> 00:18:30.470
but of course it's one of
those self-deprecating things

00:18:30.470 --> 00:18:31.530
that you only say,

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.780
if you actually think that
you are the exception.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:35.150
Right, which is definitely
what Sylvester thought

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:40.150
Sylvester's definitely was
rather proud of his verbal skill.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:42.660
And in fact, one thing he's known for

00:18:42.660 --> 00:18:46.853
is he coined a lot of mathematical
terms that we use today.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:50.870
You know, invariant, discriminant

00:18:50.870 --> 00:18:53.260
probably the most famous one is matrix.

00:18:53.260 --> 00:18:56.060
If you've ever taken linear
algebra or even algebra two

00:18:56.060 --> 00:18:58.380
in high school or like
seeing action movies,

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:01.362
like it's a word you know,
the matrix that is a coinage

00:19:01.362 --> 00:19:04.320
of Sylvester's.

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:09.090
And indeed like Hamilton,
Sylvester was a poet.

00:19:09.090 --> 00:19:12.660
He wrote what I think must be

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:17.000
the only sonnet ever addressed
to an algebraic expression,

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.870
this one to a missing member
of a family group of terms

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.250
and an algebraic formula,

00:19:21.250 --> 00:19:23.140
so I see some mathematicians in the room

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:26.590
so I think this is a poem
addressed to them on monomial.

00:19:26.590 --> 00:19:27.980
I'm not gonna read this one in full,

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:31.363
but you can like look at it
and sort of get its vibe.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:37.970
Maybe one thing I wanna say about him

00:19:37.970 --> 00:19:41.129
is that even though he is a geometer,

00:19:41.129 --> 00:19:43.440
he has a rather different view of geometry

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:45.520
than (indistinct) and Vincent Millay.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:47.400
In fact, in a stance you
might not expect them

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:48.660
at petition to have,

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:50.680
he doesn't even really
care for Euclid himself.

00:19:50.680 --> 00:19:51.680
Here's what he says,

00:19:52.960 --> 00:19:57.280
the early study of Euclid
made me a hater of geometry.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:58.790
Which I hope I may plead my excuse,

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:02.180
if I have shocked the
opinions of any in this room,

00:20:02.180 --> 00:20:03.790
he was definitely like
the 19th century version

00:20:03.790 --> 00:20:05.240
of these people.

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:07.200
Unpopular opinion that
was definitely his thought

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.013
anyway sorry, I'll continue this boat.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.010
If I have shocked the
opinions of any in this room

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:14.030
and I know there are
some who ranked Euclid

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:16.650
a second and sacredness
to the Bible alone,

00:20:16.650 --> 00:20:18.630
and that's one of the advanced outposts

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:20.030
of the British constitution.

00:20:21.070 --> 00:20:22.390
Okay, so what he's complaining about

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:27.390
is that he feels that
people are too attached

00:20:27.670 --> 00:20:31.700
to like the perfect Euclid, set in stone

00:20:31.700 --> 00:20:33.490
1,000 or more years ago.

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:37.030
He wants a geometry that is
more tied to real things,

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:39.200
more tied to the physical world

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:42.750
and he complains about
how British education

00:20:42.750 --> 00:20:44.370
is completely far behind them.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:47.560
He writes, it is this living
emphasis in the subject

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:49.890
which is so wanting our traditional

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:52.770
and medieval modes of teaching
he's talking about Britain.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:54.900
In France, Germany and Italy,

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:56.590
everywhere where I have
been on the continent

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:59.960
mind acts direct on
mind in a manner unknown

00:20:59.960 --> 00:21:03.330
the frozen formality of
our academic institutions.

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:06.970
So, Sylvester is a person
who does not want geometry

00:21:06.970 --> 00:21:08.870
to be up there far away

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:11.770
he wants to be down here with us,

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:15.160
dealing with physics,
dealing with astronomy,

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.360
dealing with the world that we observe

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:19.920
with our sensory apparatus.

00:21:19.920 --> 00:21:22.830
I would say it goes to far
as geometry is of interest

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:27.473
only in so far as a captures
reality and provides insight.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:29.833
And as for poetry,

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:35.640
he sees it as something
which like physics has laws

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:39.540
that can be divined by
reflection and experimentation,

00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:40.700
and that it's worth doing this

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:42.560
that you actually learn
something about it and understand

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:44.910
it better by understanding
its underlying laws.

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:48.190
So look, I'm not gonna
say that Sylvester's view

00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:50.750
has really taken hold as a
mode of literary criticism.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:53.473
But he did sort of sit down
and write an entire book

00:21:53.473 --> 00:21:56.550
The laws of verse,

00:21:56.550 --> 00:21:59.600
otherwise known as
principles of versification.

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:03.030
And delivered it as his opening
address when he was named

00:22:04.060 --> 00:22:07.050
the president of the
mathematical society of Britain.

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:09.017
So, this was something
he really cared about

00:22:09.017 --> 00:22:11.870
and he thought that geometric
mathematical techniques

00:22:11.870 --> 00:22:16.120
really could be brought to bare,
on the study of literature.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:19.210
And this is kind of a running
theme for better and worse

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:20.530
I'll show you an example of both.

00:22:20.530 --> 00:22:24.440
So let's move to the 20th century,

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.140
the beginning of the 20th century.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:32.150
And Andrey Markov, who was
a Russian mathematician,

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:34.620
who is one of the founders
of probability theory.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.260
And in particular,

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:39.380
the creator of what is now
called the Markov chain

00:22:39.380 --> 00:22:41.370
and you know, in a different kind of talk.

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:42.760
Well, I could give I could,

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:44.870
and in fact often do give a whole hour

00:22:44.870 --> 00:22:48.071
on the origin of the Markov
chain and the incredible bouquet

00:22:48.071 --> 00:22:51.163
of applications it has in
the contemporary world.

00:22:51.163 --> 00:22:54.380
I'm just gonna say a tiny bit about it

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:59.185
what you are looking at
is Markov's analysis.

00:22:59.185 --> 00:23:00.660
So he mentioned by the way,

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.720
I should say this to when
a theological argument

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:04.680
with another mathematician
about the nature of freewill,

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.130
a very strange story that I
thought had to be apocryphal,

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:10.030
and I researched it for the
book and it's completely true.

00:23:10.992 --> 00:23:15.992
But later he's applying it
to, the poem Eugene Onegin

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:20.490
which this is not the
way it's usually typeset,

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:23.470
but he was writing its letters one by one

00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:26.713
in a series of grids that's
what the picture you see here.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.130
And let me just briefly say,

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:32.290
what kind of analysis Markov does.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:34.740
How does he make a Markov
chain out of this poem?

00:23:35.970 --> 00:23:38.345
He says, let me throw out
everything in this poem

00:23:38.345 --> 00:23:42.480
except for the pattern
of consonants and vowels

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:45.670
and what he says that you can
sort of like model this poem,

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:46.990
please don't be mad at poets

00:23:46.990 --> 00:23:50.850
I'm gonna I'm not saying
this is what the poem is

00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:54.150
and neither is Markov but
I'll explain what I'm doing.

00:23:54.150 --> 00:23:57.290
He observed by counting a
lot of letters and grids

00:23:57.290 --> 00:23:58.690
like I showed you on the last slide,

00:23:58.690 --> 00:24:02.653
that when you had a consonant, the next,

00:24:05.180 --> 00:24:10.090
the next letter was a
vowel 66.3% of the time.

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:11.386
In other words,

00:24:11.386 --> 00:24:13.450
most of the time a consonant
was followed by a vowel

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:14.760
and not by another consonant

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:16.760
that happened only about
a third of the time.

00:24:16.760 --> 00:24:21.240
Whereas a vowel was even more
rarely followed by a vowel

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:25.790
and was much more commonly
followed by a consonant.

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:26.680
So you can imagine

00:24:26.680 --> 00:24:30.380
a kind of imitation
Eugene Onegin generator

00:24:30.380 --> 00:24:32.720
that maybe you start with some consonant

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:35.890
and then you say, okay,
I'm just gonna out random

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:37.540
two thirds of the time, pick a vowel

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:39.630
and one third of the
time pick a consonant.

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:42.610
And then if I'm at a
vowel 87.2% of the time,

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:45.900
pick a consonant and 12.8%
of the time, pick a vowel

00:24:45.900 --> 00:24:46.960
and just let that run

00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:48.870
just keep on doing that for a long time.

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:51.640
And then I'll get something
that I might call an imitation

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:52.900
of this famous poem by Pushkin.

00:24:52.900 --> 00:24:53.980
Okay, that would be like a really,

00:24:53.980 --> 00:24:55.695
really bad imitation, right?

00:24:55.695 --> 00:24:58.540
This seems like an
incredibly impoverished view

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:01.970
of what this poem could be,
but let me make the point that

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:06.060
even this incredibly minimal
amount of information

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:09.060
is enough for instance, to
distinguish two different poets.

00:25:09.060 --> 00:25:10.700
So this is what Markov does in his paper

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:13.220
the top diagram is the
diagram I just showed you

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:15.780
and the second one is a
diagram, produced from a novel

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:18.800
by a I think now mostly
forgotten Russian novelist

00:25:18.800 --> 00:25:19.717
named (indistinct).

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:24.630
And even this very meager model is enough

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:29.550
to tell the difference between
these two Russian writers.

00:25:29.550 --> 00:25:32.950
So, as I said, I'm not
gonna tell the entire story

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:36.730
of Markov chain but I'll just
comment that in modern usage,

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:40.490
you can use models which
are much more sophisticated

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:43.260
than this, but still much
less sophisticated than

00:25:43.260 --> 00:25:45.950
our actual like human linguistic faculties

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:50.520
to do a much better although
far from perfect job

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:55.350
of imitating text, mechanical production

00:25:55.350 --> 00:25:57.570
of something that kind of
imitates english language.

00:25:57.570 --> 00:25:59.130
If you've heard of (indistinct) three,

