But here's a simpler one,

00:26:05.550 --> 00:26:07.645
what I did is just train the Markov chain

00:26:07.645 --> 00:26:11.403
to imitate baby names,

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.420
just to kind of show you
what kind of information

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:19.170
can be retained by these kind
of rather simple geometric

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:20.670
models of language production.

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:24.560
You see three lists of
made up names here, right?

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:27.690
My computer made, one of them

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:32.690
I trained it to imitate baby
names from babies born in 1917.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:36.940
Another one I trained on baby
names for babies born in 1971,

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.460
which is the year I was born.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:41.170
And another one I trained on baby names

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:44.793
for babies born in 2017.

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:49.310
And I'll bet, everybody in the audience

00:26:49.310 --> 00:26:53.040
can tell which list is which,

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:55.220
which is the list that
imitates 2017 names,

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:57.430
which is the list that imitates 1917 names

00:26:57.430 --> 00:27:02.430
and which is the list that
imitates a 1971 names.

00:27:02.700 --> 00:27:05.250
So it's kind of cool, I mean
it because it's clearly,

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:07.930
some of these are names
that are actual real names,

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:09.320
some of them are names
that sound plausible,

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:10.720
some of them are names
that sound terrible.

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.150
So it's certainly not doing
the same thing humans do

00:27:13.150 --> 00:27:14.980
when we make up names for our children

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:18.470
and yet it is capturing some
interesting features of style

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:20.500
in this purely mathematical way.

00:27:20.500 --> 00:27:23.890
So I'm not gonna tell you the answers

00:27:23.890 --> 00:27:25.690
but I bet you all know,

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:29.314
but if people are not sure we
can talk about it at the end.

00:27:29.314 --> 00:27:34.314
So, and this by the way, this notion,

00:27:35.910 --> 00:27:38.030
I truly do not feel Keats might have

00:27:38.030 --> 00:27:40.660
but I do not feel that this
is an anti-literary thing

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:41.550
to think about.

00:27:41.550 --> 00:27:42.980
By con on the contrary,

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:45.180
I think there is actually
like a pretty long tradition

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:48.250
of mechanical production
of language, like writing,

00:27:48.250 --> 00:27:50.343
like the data poets used to do.

00:27:51.470 --> 00:27:54.150
In literature not as a way
to produce finished product,

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:58.180
but as a way to kind of
energize creativity as a way

00:27:58.180 --> 00:28:00.980
to understand something about
our unconscious processes

00:28:00.980 --> 00:28:02.563
of language production.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:05.590
I do wanna give one
cautionary tale I said,

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:07.033
for better or for worse,

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:12.390
somebody I wrote about in my
last book How Not Be Wrong

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:16.410
was B.F.Skinner and his
spring of Shakespeare.

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:21.410
So I quote this only in
order to object to it.

00:28:21.440 --> 00:28:25.170
But you know, he wrote a
famous paper where he's like,

00:28:25.170 --> 00:28:27.250
people say Shakespeare alliterates,

00:28:27.250 --> 00:28:29.330
but I studied the
mathematical, mathematically

00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:31.210
and I say, Shakespeare did not alliterate.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:32.350
So he was doing, I think

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:35.623
what should as my petition
should refrain from doing.

00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:42.410
We're here I mean just like
mathematical physicists

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:44.853
should refrain from telling
the universe what to do.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:50.150
We're here to understand
things about literature,

00:28:50.150 --> 00:28:52.240
not to deny things about literature

00:28:52.240 --> 00:28:54.440
that writers already understand.

00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:55.887
B.F.Skinner writes,

00:28:55.887 --> 00:28:58.240
"In spite of the seeming
richness of liberation

00:28:58.240 --> 00:28:59.700
in the sonnets and seeming richness,

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:00.907
what such power, right?"

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:04.113
Blah, blah, blah.

00:29:04.113 --> 00:29:05.540
"So far, is this aspect
of poetry is concerned

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.630
Shakespeare might as
well have drawn his words

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:08.463
out of the hat."

00:29:08.463 --> 00:29:10.800
And I have to say, when I
looked into this though,

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.400
I learned something interesting.

00:29:14.030 --> 00:29:19.030
Skinner, like Sylvester, like Hamilton

00:29:19.120 --> 00:29:20.150
wanted to be a writer

00:29:20.150 --> 00:29:21.480
that's what he wanted
to do when he was young.

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:22.630
He wanted to be a novelist actually

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:25.250
he didn't wanna write poems,
but he, he went to Bread Loaf,

00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:26.900
he got a nice letter from Robert Frost,

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:29.870
like this was his thing and
then he just didn't succeed

00:29:29.870 --> 00:29:31.110
he tried and tried to write, he didn't,

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.710
he couldn't really finish stuff.

00:29:33.596 --> 00:29:37.250
And he had a lifelong
grudge against literature

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.500
and he wrote in his journals,
literature must be destroyed

00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:40.333
He was very angry.

00:29:40.333 --> 00:29:42.340
So, I mean, is this funny
that this sort of apostle's

00:29:42.340 --> 00:29:44.200
this great apostle of rationalism,

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:47.460
you see sort of his like childhood anger

00:29:47.460 --> 00:29:52.460
kind of like leaking out into
his hostility to Shakespeare.

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:55.840
Okay, we're gonna be together,

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:57.720
at least me talking for
about 10 more minutes,

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:58.810
and then I'm gonna take questions.

00:29:58.810 --> 00:30:02.020
So let me finish off trying to bring us,

00:30:02.020 --> 00:30:03.630
we're still like in the middle
of the 20th century here.

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:08.630
So let me bring us to the
present and an actual alive poet

00:30:09.170 --> 00:30:11.060
there's will be at least one
at any point we talk, right?

00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:12.233
And this is Rita Dove,

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:15.940
who is a former poet Laureate
of the United States,

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:17.420
who is now as it happens,

00:30:17.420 --> 00:30:19.410
a professor at the University of Virginia,

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:23.277
where Sylvester beat up a
student with a sword cane

00:30:23.277 --> 00:30:25.233
about 150 years ago.

00:30:27.180 --> 00:30:28.820
And I'm gonna show you two of her poems.

00:30:28.820 --> 00:30:29.910
And actually, because we have time,

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:31.920
I think I'll read them
'cause they're great poems.

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:36.170
And I did get here fast enough to do it.

00:30:36.170 --> 00:30:39.130
So here's, you know,
most contemporary poets

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:42.030
don't even write one poem, one math poem.

00:30:42.030 --> 00:30:44.450
Dove wrote two, at least
these are the two that I know.

00:30:44.450 --> 00:30:46.053
So I'm gonna read them to you.

00:30:47.985 --> 00:30:49.985
So the first one's called "Flash Cards".

00:30:50.970 --> 00:30:55.540
In math I was the whiz kid
keeper of oranges and apples.

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:58.550
What you don't understand
master, my father said

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:01.700
the faster I answered
the faster they came.

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:05.030
I could see one bud on
the teacher's geranium.

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:07.810
One clear beast buttering at the wet pane.

00:31:07.810 --> 00:31:10.289
The tulip tree always
dragged after heavy rain.

00:31:10.289 --> 00:31:14.640
So I tucked my head as
my boots slapped home.

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:17.700
My father put up his feet after work

00:31:17.700 --> 00:31:20.630
and relaxed with a highball
and the life of Lincoln

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:25.620
after supper we drilled and I
climbed the dark before sleep

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:29.800
before a thin voice hissed
numbers as I spun on a wheel,

00:31:29.800 --> 00:31:33.963
I had to guess 10, I
kept saying I'm only 10.

00:31:35.510 --> 00:31:38.190
Okay, this is a view of math, right?

00:31:38.190 --> 00:31:40.670
A very well depicted childhood story.

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:43.530
Let me read another poem of hers,

00:31:43.530 --> 00:31:45.997
which is called "Geometry."

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:49.083
Geometry.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.780
I prove a theorem and the house expands

00:31:53.780 --> 00:31:56.390
the windows jerk free to
hover near the ceiling.

00:31:56.390 --> 00:31:59.190
The ceiling floats away with a sigh.

00:31:59.190 --> 00:32:00.710
As the walls clear themselves

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:02.590
of everything but transparency,

00:32:02.590 --> 00:32:05.500
the scent of carnations leaves with them.

00:32:05.500 --> 00:32:07.760
I'm out in the open and above

00:32:07.760 --> 00:32:10.120
the windows have hinged into butterflies,

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:12.790
sunlight glinting where
they've intersected.

00:32:12.790 --> 00:32:16.153
They are going to some
point true and unproven.

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:19.910
Okay, so two math poems.

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:21.810
I'm gonna leave this up for a minute.

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:25.100
But extremely different
in their tone, right?

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:27.480
Poems talking some sense
about the same thing,

00:32:27.480 --> 00:32:28.740
a child learning mathematics,

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:30.943
but they could not be more different.

00:32:30.943 --> 00:32:32.568
This second poem, I think is

00:32:32.568 --> 00:32:34.680
in some ways, very close to Wordsworth

00:32:34.680 --> 00:32:37.103
what Wordsworth, when he wrote
Mighty Is The Charm, right?

00:32:37.103 --> 00:32:39.440
There's sort of something about the

00:32:40.580 --> 00:32:44.510
mentally healing aspect
of studying geometry.

00:32:44.510 --> 00:32:46.800
But there is something more here, I think,

00:32:46.800 --> 00:32:48.840
than what Wordsworth was writing about.

00:32:48.840 --> 00:32:50.710
Maybe partly because Wordsworth really

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:52.390
wasn't interested in
mathematics as a child.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:53.360
He was an indifferent student.

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:55.120
He sort of was interested
in the idea of it

00:32:55.120 --> 00:32:56.770
and people like Hamilton attracted him.

00:32:56.770 --> 00:33:00.227
Dove by contrast really was
very strong in math as a child

00:33:00.227 --> 00:33:01.630
and is writing from her own experience.

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:04.840
And I think she has sort
of more of a feeling

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:05.990
for what it's actually,

00:33:07.770 --> 00:33:09.550
I think there's something really more here

00:33:09.550 --> 00:33:12.333
in the distinction between the poems.

00:33:13.650 --> 00:33:15.390
Again, I could do a whole
hour and I'm at pedagogy

00:33:15.390 --> 00:33:16.741
I'm not going to.

00:33:16.741 --> 00:33:18.490
but this distinction between memorization,

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:20.910
which is what takes
place in the first poem

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:22.570
and understanding,

00:33:22.570 --> 00:33:24.990
which just takes place
in the second, right?

00:33:24.990 --> 00:33:25.880
In the first poem,

00:33:25.880 --> 00:33:28.620
it doesn't really seem like
her father is asking her

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:30.670
to understand something about arithmetic.

00:33:30.670 --> 00:33:31.790
He's drilling her.

00:33:31.790 --> 00:33:36.230
In the second poem, this
thing I just noticed actually,

00:33:36.230 --> 00:33:37.920
I heard this poem about 100 times by now,

00:33:37.920 --> 00:33:42.341
but like there is no authority
figure in this poem, right?

00:33:42.341 --> 00:33:44.670
In real life, there would presumably

00:33:44.670 --> 00:33:47.002
be a teacher in the
room where this happens.

00:33:47.002 --> 00:33:49.720
Well, maybe she's in
school, maybe he's at home.

00:33:49.720 --> 00:33:52.250
But if she's at home, the
father is not in the poem.

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:54.540
If she's at school, the teacher
is not in the poem, right?

00:33:54.540 --> 00:33:57.190
It's just her, just her and the geometry.

00:33:57.190 --> 00:34:01.010
And this is something about
what makes geometry special

00:34:01.010 --> 00:34:03.640
that makes it jump out
from everything else

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:07.410
in the young student curriculum

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:11.233
that you can make knowledge by yourself.

00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:15.290
You don't need a parent
or a teacher to tell you,

00:34:15.290 --> 00:34:17.110
you don't need to rely
on a book that tells you

00:34:17.110 --> 00:34:19.930
what happened long before you were alive.

00:34:19.930 --> 00:34:22.230
You can start from the
basic rules and construct

00:34:22.230 --> 00:34:24.940
your own knowledge, independent
of any authority figure

00:34:24.940 --> 00:34:26.760
to the point that as in this poem,

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:28.360
the authority figures completely banishes.

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:32.430
And this is, this feature
of geometry is why

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:37.030
geometry has often been seen as a threat

00:34:37.030 --> 00:34:38.823
to the established order of things.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:43.900
I didn't put this in my notes,

00:34:43.900 --> 00:34:45.810
but it just occurs to be
mentioned that you know

00:34:45.810 --> 00:34:48.340
Newton, we talked about who
likes him, who hates him.

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:50.630
He was certainly seen as a threat

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:52.340
by religious establishment
and there was like,

00:34:52.340 --> 00:34:54.610
he adored his defenders,
wrote books with titles

00:34:54.610 --> 00:34:57.363
like Geometry has no
friends to infidelity.

00:34:58.420 --> 00:34:59.253
You don't write that book

00:34:59.253 --> 00:35:01.730
unless somebody's saying
the opposite, right?

00:35:01.730 --> 00:35:05.790
So this idea of geometry
as a kind of liberation

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:07.852
that I think is very contemporary

00:35:07.852 --> 00:35:09.750
I don't think it's really
there in the 19th century

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:11.223
those we talked about.

00:35:12.240 --> 00:35:13.760
Except maybe in one place,

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:16.890
so sorry for the bad
reproduction of this picture.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:20.090
But this is a picture from
a book called Flatland

00:35:20.090 --> 00:35:21.140
I don't know if you guys have read it.

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:22.640
We're back in the 19th
century in England now

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:25.950
it's a book by Edwin Abbott,

00:35:25.950 --> 00:35:27.560
whose middle name was also
Abbott added by the way,

00:35:27.560 --> 00:35:28.757
Edwin Abbott Abbott.

00:35:32.650 --> 00:35:35.330
And let me just briefly tell
you what the story is about

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:36.920
and if you haven't read
it, it's kind of charming

00:35:36.920 --> 00:35:37.753
I recommend it.

00:35:38.611 --> 00:35:40.500
The book is called Flatland

00:35:40.500 --> 00:35:43.120
because it takes place in
a two dimensional world

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:45.210
and the main character is a Square.

00:35:45.210 --> 00:35:48.101
Everyone in this world is
a two dimensional figure

00:35:48.101 --> 00:35:51.426
(indistinct) determined
by how many sides you have

00:35:51.426 --> 00:35:54.980
with, you know, triangles
as the working class,

00:35:54.980 --> 00:35:55.820
square it's a little better,

00:35:55.820 --> 00:35:58.873
hexagons very fancy,
circles are like the best.

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:03.250
And one day the square
was kind of in this house

00:36:03.250 --> 00:36:08.250
when a circle, a very small
circle appears in his house.

00:36:08.700 --> 00:36:09.670
He's like, oh my God there's like

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:12.100
some like baby aristocrat
here, like what's going on?

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:13.540
And then something very strange happens.

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:15.770
The circle starts
getting bigger and bigger

00:36:17.360 --> 00:36:20.375
going before his eyes
and the square is like,

00:36:20.375 --> 00:36:23.960
what uncanny nonsense is this,
like, this makes no sense

00:36:23.960 --> 00:36:24.980
this cannot be happening.

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:26.570
And of course, well you
can figure what's happening

00:36:26.570 --> 00:36:28.330
because I showed you a picture of it.

00:36:28.330 --> 00:36:30.670
He is being visited by a being

00:36:30.670 --> 00:36:33.520
from the three-dimensional
world, a sphere.

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:36.840
And as the sphere starts to enter,

00:36:36.840 --> 00:36:39.970
his flat land moves more fully into it

00:36:39.970 --> 00:36:44.610
and finally immerses itself
totally in the squares,

00:36:44.610 --> 00:36:46.800
two dimensional world so that the plane

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.600
is cutting the sphere at it's a crater.

00:36:48.600 --> 00:36:50.940
It appears to the square
to get bigger and bigger

00:36:50.940 --> 00:36:53.040
that's the only way he can interpret

00:36:53.040 --> 00:36:54.540
this three-dimensional motion.

00:36:55.713 --> 00:36:58.943
And so then what follows,
this very long argument

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:04.370
where the sphere is trying
to explain to the square,

00:37:04.370 --> 00:37:05.840
what the third dimension is

00:37:05.840 --> 00:37:08.538
and square just keeps on being
like, but it makes no sense

00:37:08.538 --> 00:37:10.680
I mean, you're saying it,

00:37:10.680 --> 00:37:13.150
but you're talking about
something that doesn't exist.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:16.780
And finally, the sphere is
able to convince the square.

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:18.740
The sphere says in my world,

00:37:18.740 --> 00:37:22.033
there's a being that's a lot
like you called the cube.

00:37:23.020 --> 00:37:25.550
It's just like you
except three-dimensional.

00:37:25.550 --> 00:37:28.852
And he's able to sort of help
the square to reason about

00:37:28.852 --> 00:37:30.660
the fact that if there
were a being like him,

00:37:30.660 --> 00:37:31.980
but three-dimensional,

00:37:31.980 --> 00:37:36.980
it would have to have
eight vertices and 12 edges

00:37:38.420 --> 00:37:40.930
and six faces each of which
was the square like him.

00:37:40.930 --> 00:37:44.310
And the square, just purely
through reason, you know,

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:49.163
they go back and forth a bit
is able to grasp this sort of,

00:37:50.148 --> 00:37:52.790
actually I told this story
in a bit in the wrong order,

00:37:52.790 --> 00:37:54.170
I would love it if it was just purely

00:37:54.170 --> 00:37:55.003
an exercise of reasoning.

00:37:55.003 --> 00:37:58.050
But in fact, what happens is
the sphere grabs the square

00:37:58.050 --> 00:37:59.480
and pulls him out of his world

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:03.133
so that he can see his world from above.

00:38:04.950 --> 00:38:06.510
And then the square really believes it,

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:08.037
the square is like whereas like okay,

00:38:08.037 --> 00:38:09.833
I've been to the third
dimension, I can see it.

00:38:13.960 --> 00:38:16.810
But here's what happens next
is sort of an ironic moment.

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:19.580
We can talk about a whole thing about like

00:38:19.580 --> 00:38:22.563
the religious imagery here,
but I think I won't do it.

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:28.990
The square having had this revelation,

00:38:28.990 --> 00:38:31.490
having been supplied this
revelation by the sphere

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:35.530
is very excited and it's like, now get it,

00:38:35.530 --> 00:38:37.490
now at last I understand what
you were trying to tell me.

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:41.450
Now I understand why a
three-dimensional version of me

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:43.410
would have to have these
properties, even though I don't

00:38:43.410 --> 00:38:46.080
really have the sensory
apparatus to perceive it

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:47.270
I can figure it out by reason.

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:49.530
And what's more now I understand

00:38:49.530 --> 00:38:52.230
what the four dimensional
version of me would be like.

00:38:54.120 --> 00:38:56.400
That it would have faces
for themselves cubes

00:38:56.400 --> 00:38:58.870
and it would have like edges
and it would have 16 vertices,

00:38:58.870 --> 00:39:00.150
like now I understand everything.

00:39:00.150 --> 00:39:02.110
And the sphere is like, what
the hell are you talking about?

00:39:02.110 --> 00:39:03.830
There's no fourth dimension,
like that's ridiculous

00:39:03.830 --> 00:39:05.540
like 3D is how many dimensions there are

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:07.180
were you not listening to anything I said?

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:09.440
So it's this, it's this
rather arch moment,

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:11.990
sorry for the spoilers,
but this book is from 1881,

00:39:13.630 --> 00:39:15.370
where even the sphere is sort of like

00:39:15.370 --> 00:39:18.762
locked into its prison,
the perception and the,

00:39:18.762 --> 00:39:20.730
and then the square is able to go past.

00:39:20.730 --> 00:39:24.300
But again, it's because
of the power of geometry

00:39:24.300 --> 00:39:26.270
and because of the power
of mathematical reason,

00:39:26.270 --> 00:39:30.400
if the square is able to
break free from this prison

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.320
of what it is able to see.

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:36.963
Deliberate itself from the
authorities of its own world.

00:39:38.160 --> 00:39:43.053
So back to the present,

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:49.530
what do we think geometry is now

00:39:53.790 --> 00:39:54.930
what I'm gonna close,

00:39:54.930 --> 00:39:57.340
because we're here at this kind of nexus

00:39:57.340 --> 00:40:00.770
of science and humanities
is I wanna emphasize

00:40:00.770 --> 00:40:03.230
what I think is really
most present in Dove

00:40:03.230 --> 00:40:04.980
out of all that we've talked about.

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:07.770
The geometry, like all of mathematics

00:40:07.770 --> 00:40:10.210
is fundamentally a human activity.

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:12.460
It's something humans
do for human purposes.

00:40:12.460 --> 00:40:14.750
And so I, you know, what
I like to say is that math

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:16.113
is made of people.

00:40:17.280 --> 00:40:19.083
So as much as there's an aspect of it,

00:40:19.083 --> 00:40:22.640
that we may feel is like beyond
humanity, beyond ourselves,

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:25.530
like transcendent relating to
something that exists before

00:40:25.530 --> 00:40:27.750
humanity was here, not
that humanity is gone,

00:40:27.750 --> 00:40:32.110
math as actually practiced
is something we do

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:33.500
because it's something humans wanna do

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:34.670
and we do it for our own reasons.

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:36.900
And I think that's
certainly present in Dove,

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:38.960
but it's also present in
contemporary mathematicians.

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:41.320
Let me quote a wonderful
quote from Bill Thurston,

00:40:41.320 --> 00:40:43.610
who I think anyone would say

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:45.550
is one of the great geometers of our time.

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:48.533
Now, unfortunately
deceased a few years back.

00:40:50.730 --> 00:40:53.007
He said about math,

00:40:53.007 --> 00:40:56.650
"We are not trying to meet
some abstract production quota

00:40:56.650 --> 00:40:59.670
of definitions, theorems and proofs.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:01.970
The measure of our success
is whether what we do

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:05.690
enables people to understand
and think more clearly

00:41:05.690 --> 00:41:08.910
and effectively about mathematics."

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:10.790
This is a point that I think is often lost

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:11.623
to people outside of mathematics.

00:41:11.623 --> 00:41:13.930
That our job is not, or
what we're interested in

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:15.873
is not what's true or false.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:20.024
What's interested in,
what we're interested in

00:41:20.024 --> 00:41:21.820
is humans increasing their understanding

00:41:21.820 --> 00:41:23.380
of what is in the world,

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:24.960
where that might be the physical world,

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:26.910
or it might be the abstract
mathematical world.

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:28.613
David Blackwell, who was a probabilist

00:41:28.613 --> 00:41:31.020
like Markov and other
contemporary mathematician,

00:41:31.020 --> 00:41:32.530
he put it really bluntly actually.

00:41:32.530 --> 00:41:34.170
I think things very close to Thurston.

00:41:34.170 --> 00:41:36.190
He said, basically,

00:41:36.190 --> 00:41:38.920
I'm not interested in doing
research and I never have been.

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:40.770
Wait, like, imagine that in
your grant proposal, right?

00:41:40.770 --> 00:41:41.980
Like we don't say it
in our grant proposal,

00:41:41.980 --> 00:41:44.550
but sometimes if we're
being honest, we say it.

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:47.850
I'm not interested in doing
research and I never have been.

00:41:47.850 --> 00:41:50.340
I'm interested in understanding,

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:52.690
which is quite a different thing.

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:55.620
And I think it's very clear
that that's what Dove is too.

00:41:55.620 --> 00:41:58.570
That Dove correctly
understands that interest

00:41:58.570 --> 00:42:00.300
to view what mathematics is about.

00:42:00.300 --> 00:42:02.560
You know, so there is
this stereotype I think.

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:04.880
It's what Sylvester
called frozen formality.

00:42:04.880 --> 00:42:08.090
That what geometry is about
is locking our intuition

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:11.600
in this straight jacket of rigid proofs.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:14.240
I think Keats certainly thought that way,

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:16.740
but I think the way that
modern mathematicians

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:20.122
like Thurston and Blackwell
see it is much closer

00:42:20.122 --> 00:42:22.900
to what Dove is trying to get at.

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:25.850
Closer to the way I see it if
you want my personal opinion.

00:42:27.270 --> 00:42:29.780
That it's about getting to
a point in Dove's words,

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.530
that is true and unproven.

