everybody's gonna see it
through their own lens

00:51:43.060 --> 00:51:45.743
and see a different aspect of it.

00:51:46.650 --> 00:51:49.090
But that certainly is
like part of my process.

00:51:49.090 --> 00:51:50.602
Oh, I have like.

00:51:50.602 --> 00:51:51.435
- Yeah, there are many questions

00:51:51.435 --> 00:51:53.220
that have started coming in now.

00:51:53.220 --> 00:51:55.450
- I can read one out if you'd like.

00:51:55.450 --> 00:51:56.950
- You did it Priya, okay.

00:51:56.950 --> 00:51:58.900
- Okay, from Erin,

00:51:58.900 --> 00:52:01.590
you mentioned that
geometry is often the thing

00:52:01.590 --> 00:52:04.810
that prompts poets to see
beauty liberation, et cetera,

00:52:04.810 --> 00:52:06.510
in mathematics.

00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:09.670
Do you think that there's a
specific thing about geometry

00:52:09.670 --> 00:52:12.920
that does this, or is
it that students only,

00:52:12.920 --> 00:52:16.650
or at least first exposure
to proof based math

00:52:16.650 --> 00:52:18.770
is through geometry?

00:52:18.770 --> 00:52:20.170
- Yeah, awesome question.

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:22.103
And I feel like I sort
of set myself up for it

00:52:22.103 --> 00:52:24.030
with those two poems of Rita Dove

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:25.690
where she like cramps on arithmetic

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:27.650
and then there's like,
but geometry is great.

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:32.150
And it's absolutely true
that in a different world

00:52:32.150 --> 00:52:33.600
where everything was taught differently,

00:52:33.600 --> 00:52:35.330
that experience you had with geometry,

00:52:35.330 --> 00:52:39.550
she could have had with arithmetic
and the theory of numbers

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:41.620
like that certainly could have happened.

00:52:41.620 --> 00:52:46.620
In our world, there's
this very long tradition

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:51.170
of somehow seeing Euclid's
geometry as primal.

00:52:51.170 --> 00:52:53.720
Like the sort of true thing that everyone,

00:52:53.720 --> 00:52:55.650
that everything else is kind of like a

00:52:55.650 --> 00:52:59.060
slightly, dirty imitation of.

00:52:59.060 --> 00:53:00.240
So I think there's something cultural

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:01.929
and probably like Western cultural

00:53:01.929 --> 00:53:05.320
that causes people to reach for Euclid

00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:07.580
as this figure of purity that causes,

00:53:07.580 --> 00:53:09.550
you know, why doesn't Edison

00:53:09.550 --> 00:53:11.870
say like only dive fantasy alone

00:53:11.870 --> 00:53:13.430
has looked on beauty bear.

00:53:13.430 --> 00:53:14.263
You know what I mean?

00:53:14.263 --> 00:53:16.173
I mean, dive fantasy is great, but like,

00:53:18.370 --> 00:53:20.110
so I think it's culturally bound

00:53:20.110 --> 00:53:22.850
and I don't think it's
intrinsic to the subject

00:53:22.850 --> 00:53:26.210
and to be frank, the
lines between geometry

00:53:26.210 --> 00:53:29.280
and the rest of math are not too firm.

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:31.470
And I think a very
Catholic view in my book

00:53:31.470 --> 00:53:33.430
and, you know, honestly, some
people complain about it.

00:53:33.430 --> 00:53:35.370
They're like I wanted more triangles.

00:53:35.370 --> 00:53:36.390
I frankly feel cheated.

00:53:36.390 --> 00:53:37.290
I bought this book

00:53:38.358 --> 00:53:39.490
and then there were like
stuff about Markov chains

00:53:39.490 --> 00:53:41.540
and stuff about, you know,
legislative districting

00:53:41.540 --> 00:53:43.510
and like all kinds of stuff that like,

00:53:43.510 --> 00:53:46.640
it's true I have a very
broad view of what counts as,

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:47.563
as geometric.

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:53.470
- So there's another question, you know,

00:53:53.470 --> 00:53:56.633
how can geometry help in the
fight against gerrymandering?

00:53:57.901 --> 00:54:00.067
- Great question.

00:54:00.067 --> 00:54:01.550
And I write a lot about it in the book

00:54:01.550 --> 00:54:03.010
because I live in Wisconsin,

00:54:03.010 --> 00:54:04.270
which has some of the most

00:54:04.270 --> 00:54:06.060
gerrymandered legislative
districts in the country.

00:54:06.060 --> 00:54:08.560
That's how I learned about this stuff

00:54:08.560 --> 00:54:10.570
with the help of my colleague, Moon Duchin

00:54:10.570 --> 00:54:12.450
mathematician, and a geometer at Tufts,

00:54:12.450 --> 00:54:14.254
who's really been on
the front line of this.

00:54:14.254 --> 00:54:17.340
Again, I could give a
whole hour on, I often do,

00:54:17.340 --> 00:54:19.230
but let me give this sort
of three minute version,

00:54:19.230 --> 00:54:22.010
which is to say it
doesn't help by telling us

00:54:22.010 --> 00:54:25.300
how to draw congressional district.

00:54:25.300 --> 00:54:27.270
It doesn't help by giving
the answer and saying,

00:54:27.270 --> 00:54:28.740
what's the right thing to do?

00:54:28.740 --> 00:54:31.950
Because that is fundamentally
not a math question.

00:54:31.950 --> 00:54:34.560
It has math in it, but
it's a political question.

00:54:34.560 --> 00:54:36.470
What it can do,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:38.330
I always say, it can't tell
you the right way to do it,

00:54:38.330 --> 00:54:40.290
but it can tell you about
wrong ways to do it.

00:54:40.290 --> 00:54:42.900
It can identify like
really grievous offenses

00:54:42.900 --> 00:54:46.050
against fairness and just kind of like

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:50.820
put a fence around the most
egregious gerrymandering

00:54:50.820 --> 00:54:52.610
that helps create some space for reform.

00:54:52.610 --> 00:54:54.980
And I think, I mean, if you guys,

00:54:54.980 --> 00:54:56.560
I don't know how many
people in the audience

00:54:56.560 --> 00:54:58.500
are here in the U.S. and
are following U.S. politics,

00:54:58.500 --> 00:55:00.000
but this is gonna be a big fight

00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:01.210
over the next several months.

00:55:01.210 --> 00:55:04.510
It's already happening in some
states, including Wisconsin.

00:55:04.510 --> 00:55:07.520
And absolutely there are a
lot of mathematicians who are

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:11.570
providing real time assessments of

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:12.810
the maps that people are drawing

00:55:12.810 --> 00:55:15.653
and helping to shine a
light on what's being done.

00:55:17.590 --> 00:55:18.423
But I'm not doing it.

00:55:18.423 --> 00:55:20.280
I just fight about the people who do it.

00:55:21.642 --> 00:55:23.380
- I have another question to read out.

00:55:23.380 --> 00:55:26.270
Do you have anything to
say about Lewis Carroll,

00:55:26.270 --> 00:55:28.600
the participant and I
agree I've heard this too,

00:55:28.600 --> 00:55:30.300
that Alison Wonderland has a lot of

00:55:30.300 --> 00:55:32.860
hidden and unhidden mathematics.

00:55:32.860 --> 00:55:35.510
- Yes, there's actually like,
you know, in a director's cut,

00:55:35.510 --> 00:55:37.750
there's like a deleted scene
where I wrote a bit about

00:55:37.750 --> 00:55:40.370
Lewis Carroll because he was
very interested in Euclid

00:55:40.370 --> 00:55:42.090
and he had some,

00:55:42.090 --> 00:55:44.900
I took this out 'cause I
thought it got a little bit

00:55:44.900 --> 00:55:46.990
too into the weeds and
an early part of the book

00:55:46.990 --> 00:55:49.010
where I was like writing
about Euclid and his geometry.

00:55:49.010 --> 00:55:53.516
But, you know, we have
this stereotype of like,

00:55:53.516 --> 00:55:58.450
okay, Euclid, like, that's it perfection

00:55:58.450 --> 00:56:01.257
300 AD and it's all been
downhill from there, right?

00:56:01.257 --> 00:56:03.740
That's this like, very, like,

00:56:03.740 --> 00:56:05.950
it's kind of a bad
cultural habit that we have

00:56:05.950 --> 00:56:09.460
of somehow thinking like
everything was perfect in Greece

00:56:09.460 --> 00:56:11.040
and then everything since
then is like a sort of like

00:56:11.040 --> 00:56:13.600
bad carbon copy of a carbon copy of that.

00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:16.100
In fact, Euclid by modern standards,

00:56:16.100 --> 00:56:18.870
even though like somebody
like Vincent Millay

00:56:18.870 --> 00:56:19.850
would have been told in school,

00:56:19.850 --> 00:56:21.570
this is it, the high point of rigor

00:56:21.570 --> 00:56:24.350
and beauty and perfection is not rigorous

00:56:24.350 --> 00:56:25.810
by modern mathematical standards.

00:56:25.810 --> 00:56:27.630
And in fact, the axioms that we talk about

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:29.370
as Euclidean axioms today,

00:56:29.370 --> 00:56:31.780
they were cleaned up in
the early 20th century.

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:35.010
We usually use ones,
there's a set by Hilbert.

00:56:35.010 --> 00:56:37.240
There's a set by somebody
else whose name escapes me.

00:56:37.240 --> 00:56:40.090
They're like, because Euclid
was really not up to the task

00:56:40.090 --> 00:56:42.260
of what we now consider rigorous.

00:56:42.260 --> 00:56:47.260
So Carroll, he kind of
had a critical essay

00:56:48.550 --> 00:56:52.080
where he's like, come on, like
every English school child

00:56:52.080 --> 00:56:53.380
learns this proof by Euclid

00:56:53.380 --> 00:56:54.213
because it's what Euclid said

00:56:54.213 --> 00:56:55.460
and it like makes no sense,

00:56:55.460 --> 00:56:57.080
like there's a step that's
completely messed up.

00:56:57.080 --> 00:56:59.120
And he's sort of like wrote his own,

00:56:59.120 --> 00:57:03.200
which at that time was a sort
of pretty bold thing to do,

00:57:03.200 --> 00:57:04.420
but nowadays will be commonplace.

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:05.253
Like everybody knows,

00:57:05.253 --> 00:57:07.190
like, yeah, like, I mean,
it's actually, okay.

00:57:07.190 --> 00:57:08.270
It's an interesting pedagogical thing

00:57:08.270 --> 00:57:09.570
because there are places,

00:57:09.570 --> 00:57:10.940
I have a wonderful colleague at St. John's

00:57:10.940 --> 00:57:12.060
and we always fight about this.

00:57:12.060 --> 00:57:14.460
They learn geometry out of Euclid,

00:57:14.460 --> 00:57:16.960
which I think is a terrible mistake.

00:57:16.960 --> 00:57:19.537
But I mean, there's a
certain sort of habit of mind

00:57:19.537 --> 00:57:20.370
that says like,

00:57:20.370 --> 00:57:22.280
well, of course you should
like go to the source,

00:57:22.280 --> 00:57:25.130
but that's not what I think.

00:57:25.130 --> 00:57:27.610
Euclid's incredibly
historical and important.

00:57:27.610 --> 00:57:28.443
I just don't think

00:57:28.443 --> 00:57:30.013
that's where you should
learn geometry now.

00:57:32.460 --> 00:57:33.293
- Thanks Jordan.

00:57:33.293 --> 00:57:34.908
Okay, next question.

00:57:34.908 --> 00:57:36.250
Participant says,

00:57:36.250 --> 00:57:39.360
I've read your newest book,
Shape and really enjoyed it.

00:57:39.360 --> 00:57:40.980
Two-part question from this.

00:57:40.980 --> 00:57:44.570
I'm curious what your
favorite mathematics book is

00:57:44.570 --> 00:57:47.623
and what other books I should
have on my reading radar.

00:57:48.900 --> 00:57:50.680
He knows I also work at a bookshop

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:52.870
and tell every customer about your book.

00:57:52.870 --> 00:57:56.883
He's also pursuing his bachelor's
in applied mathematics.

00:57:57.811 --> 00:57:59.431
- Everything you do I love.

00:57:59.431 --> 00:58:01.070
I love that you're selling my book,

00:58:01.070 --> 00:58:03.130
I love that you're studying math.

00:58:03.130 --> 00:58:05.680
I can't say that I have a single favorite.

00:58:05.680 --> 00:58:06.890
I'll say that

00:58:06.890 --> 00:58:10.010
one it's very inspirational
to me as a writer.

00:58:10.010 --> 00:58:11.684
And I don't even know if
most people would consider it

00:58:11.684 --> 00:58:14.090
a math book.

00:58:14.090 --> 00:58:17.110
Is the book Godel Escher
Bach by Douglas Hofstadter.

00:58:17.110 --> 00:58:18.494
It's an old book.

00:58:18.494 --> 00:58:20.870
I read it when I was a kid,
probably about 50 times.

00:58:20.870 --> 00:58:22.190
And you know, when I was a younger kid,

00:58:22.190 --> 00:58:23.900
I really didn't understand very much of it

00:58:23.900 --> 00:58:24.870
and I skipped a lot of it

00:58:24.870 --> 00:58:26.220
and just like each time I would read it,

00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:27.470
I would understand more.

00:58:27.470 --> 00:58:29.245
But I think if you know the book

00:58:29.245 --> 00:58:32.660
it's kind of this meditation
on like ideas of self-reference

00:58:35.268 --> 00:58:36.540
and ideas of recursion,

00:58:36.540 --> 00:58:39.268
and it kind of ties
together like mathematics,

00:58:39.268 --> 00:58:43.510
theoretical computer science,
artificial intelligence,

00:58:43.510 --> 00:58:46.730
music, and art, and nobody
else was doing that.

00:58:46.730 --> 00:58:48.620
Nobody else was doing anything like that.

00:58:48.620 --> 00:58:50.450
And I just found it so inspiring

00:58:50.450 --> 00:58:53.150
that you were allowed to do that,

00:58:53.150 --> 00:58:55.960
that you were allowed to be
like, not just like physics,

00:58:55.960 --> 00:58:59.920
like math touches everything
and just to go his playfulness

00:58:59.920 --> 00:59:01.700
and his willingness to sort of

00:59:01.700 --> 00:59:04.563
be extremely serious, but not stuffy,

00:59:05.940 --> 00:59:09.510
that's kind of a model for me.

00:59:09.510 --> 00:59:10.554
If you looked at the,

00:59:10.554 --> 00:59:11.387
but I don't know if you
would think his style

00:59:11.387 --> 00:59:12.220
was like my style,

00:59:12.220 --> 00:59:13.180
but it sort of inspirational to me.

00:59:13.180 --> 00:59:16.440
To be quite honest, the
other, I mean, yeah,

00:59:16.440 --> 00:59:18.670
no, I think that's, that's as
good an answer as I can give.

00:59:18.670 --> 00:59:21.410
I mean, there's a lot of, we're
in a, sort of a golden age

00:59:21.410 --> 00:59:25.030
of popular math writing and
like tons of great stuff out

00:59:25.030 --> 00:59:26.890
Steve Strogatz's books are all great.

00:59:26.890 --> 00:59:28.790
Those are like ones that come to mind.

00:59:30.030 --> 00:59:31.920
- He's spoken in our series before.

00:59:31.920 --> 00:59:33.140
- Oh, okay.

00:59:33.140 --> 00:59:34.100
Why do you ask him first?

00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:35.332
No, I'm just kidding.

00:59:35.332 --> 00:59:37.850
He's older than me.

00:59:38.710 --> 00:59:42.530
- Well, I think is this
is when his book came out.

00:59:42.530 --> 00:59:43.640
- Oh, yeah okay.

00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:47.290
- We tend to invite people soon after,

00:59:47.290 --> 00:59:50.240
as soon as after we can get
them when their books come out.

00:59:53.550 --> 00:59:54.850
Okay, so there's a question

00:59:54.850 --> 00:59:57.700
from one of our Frankie
fellows that I'll read out.

00:59:57.700 --> 01:00:00.490
Before Wordsworth and Keats James Thompson

01:00:00.490 --> 01:00:03.300
and Alexander Pope were
famously sparring inverse

01:00:03.300 --> 01:00:05.493
over the poetic value of Newtonian optics

01:00:05.493 --> 01:00:08.000
and enlightenment sense more generally

01:00:08.000 --> 01:00:09.600
a science more generally.

01:00:09.600 --> 01:00:12.340
From the early 20th century
into the present day,

01:00:12.340 --> 01:00:14.920
countless writers have
registered conflicting feelings

01:00:14.920 --> 01:00:17.690
about Einsteinian space-time geometry.

01:00:17.690 --> 01:00:19.870
A system at once of staggering beauty

01:00:19.870 --> 01:00:23.130
and almost unthinkable
destructive potential.

01:00:23.130 --> 01:00:26.890
As we continue to debate the
social responsibility entailed,

01:00:26.890 --> 01:00:29.300
for example, an epidemiology models

01:00:29.300 --> 01:00:31.150
or mathematical climate models,

01:00:31.150 --> 01:00:33.440
are we rehashing the same old debate

01:00:33.440 --> 01:00:36.330
as the one you highlight
between Keats and Wordsworth,

01:00:36.330 --> 01:00:38.350
or do you notice any interesting shifts

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:39.804
across the historical record,

01:00:39.804 --> 01:00:41.780
around the complicated position

01:00:41.780 --> 01:00:43.723
of maths and science in culture?

01:00:45.150 --> 01:00:46.170
- It's a great question

01:00:46.170 --> 01:00:48.730
partly because I'm learning a lot from it.

01:00:48.730 --> 01:00:51.387
I hope I don't get like fired
from Frankie for saying this,

01:00:51.387 --> 01:00:53.740
but I don't even know
who James Thompson is.

01:00:53.740 --> 01:00:55.030
I know who Alexander Pope is.

01:00:55.030 --> 01:00:57.930
So as you see,

01:00:57.930 --> 01:01:01.580
I have like a somewhat limited view

01:01:01.580 --> 01:01:03.240
and don't know about this sparring.

01:01:03.240 --> 01:01:05.040
In fact, I strongly encourage you

01:01:05.040 --> 01:01:06.580
if you're willing to like email me

01:01:06.580 --> 01:01:08.070
and sort of tell me
where I should read about

01:01:08.070 --> 01:01:08.970
Thompson and Pope,

01:01:08.970 --> 01:01:11.900
because I know there's
like a large history

01:01:11.900 --> 01:01:13.300
of which I know only a part,

01:01:16.310 --> 01:01:19.253
but to the question, to the question,

01:01:23.680 --> 01:01:27.831
I don't think I see that exact
Keats, Wordsworth division

01:01:27.831 --> 01:01:31.810
when it comes to things like
climate models for instance,

01:01:31.810 --> 01:01:34.400
because I don't think there's like

01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:37.093
anyone who takes the stance that,

01:01:38.210 --> 01:01:40.780
oh, we're just not fully capturing

01:01:40.780 --> 01:01:42.810
like the glorious richness of the climate

01:01:42.810 --> 01:01:45.831
by reducing it to questions about

01:01:45.831 --> 01:01:49.620
what the average global temperature is.

01:01:49.620 --> 01:01:51.330
Right, man, I mean, you could certainly

01:01:51.330 --> 01:01:53.850
find people saying that that's productive,

01:01:53.850 --> 01:01:56.600
but I think everybody grasps

01:01:56.600 --> 01:01:59.290
that that's an important
number that captures

01:01:59.290 --> 01:02:03.220
an important feature and is
not like missing the point.

01:02:03.220 --> 01:02:04.891
That's what I would take to be the kind of

01:02:04.891 --> 01:02:06.770
cutesy and view, right?

01:02:06.770 --> 01:02:08.620
If there was sort of somebody
out there who was like,

01:02:08.620 --> 01:02:09.870
oh, all these people are like

01:02:09.870 --> 01:02:12.360
doing all these hardcore
numerical analysis models

01:02:12.360 --> 01:02:15.430
of the climate, like, why
can't they just like, sort of

01:02:15.430 --> 01:02:17.450
sit and enjoy like the waves,

01:02:17.450 --> 01:02:18.627
like coming down the street
or something like that?

01:02:18.627 --> 01:02:20.030
Right, that's not a view.

01:02:20.030 --> 01:02:22.830
I think that is like, well,
maybe I'm being too specific,

01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:24.030
but here's where I,

01:02:24.030 --> 01:02:25.599
okay, let me take it
a different direction.

01:02:25.599 --> 01:02:29.095
The place where I think maybe I see it

01:02:29.095 --> 01:02:32.810
is in these questions people ask

01:02:32.810 --> 01:02:36.340
about the algorithmised self.

01:02:36.340 --> 01:02:37.550
Like people who are like, okay,

01:02:37.550 --> 01:02:40.480
does like Facebook know me and my friends

01:02:40.480 --> 01:02:42.830
better than we know ourselves?

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:44.500
Right, does the algorithm

01:02:44.500 --> 01:02:46.500
that sort of can track
your internet usage,

01:02:46.500 --> 01:02:51.340
does it have some kind of insight into you

01:02:52.520 --> 01:02:55.300
that is in some sense
deeper or at least different

01:02:57.079 --> 01:02:59.030
than what humans have?

01:02:59.030 --> 01:03:01.530
Yeah, so actually I think that's
the place where you see it,

01:03:01.530 --> 01:03:04.350
that, and the sort of similar
question of like what,

01:03:04.350 --> 01:03:06.400
you know, what's happening
with machine learning

01:03:06.400 --> 01:03:09.400
and what's happening with AI.

01:03:09.400 --> 01:03:10.267
Like if we sort of say like,

01:03:10.267 --> 01:03:13.440
and I do write about this
in the book, like, you know,

01:03:13.440 --> 01:03:16.313
if an AI plays chess very
well, which indeed it does,

01:03:17.944 --> 01:03:22.190
is that ruining chest the
way Keats accused Newton

01:03:22.190 --> 01:03:27.100
of ruining the rainbow or
does that enrich chess?

01:03:27.100 --> 01:03:29.590
And that is the sort of
hotly contested question,

01:03:29.590 --> 01:03:31.690
certainly among chess people.

01:03:31.690 --> 01:03:32.800
Is that an okay answer?

01:03:32.800 --> 01:03:33.633
Do you feel like Tyler,

01:03:33.633 --> 01:03:36.910
do you feel like I answered
the question you were asking?

01:03:36.910 --> 01:03:38.705
But I took it away from the
things of real political import

01:03:38.705 --> 01:03:40.280
epidemiology and climate.

01:03:40.280 --> 01:03:42.743
So maybe it's not the direction
you wanted to go with it.

01:03:45.060 --> 01:03:48.423
- Let me wait and see
what Tyler has to say.

01:03:50.450 --> 01:03:52.060
- Okay, I got some word for this,

01:03:52.060 --> 01:03:54.090
but please do write me
about Pope and Thompson

01:03:54.090 --> 01:03:56.283
'cause I would love to know.

01:03:59.370 --> 01:04:01.903
- So are there any more questions?

01:04:06.440 --> 01:04:08.410
Did we, Ty, did you take a look?

01:04:08.410 --> 01:04:10.628
We didn't miss any comments or questions.

01:04:10.628 --> 01:04:12.378
- I looked, I didn't see any other.

01:04:15.513 --> 01:04:18.480
- Well, let's just wait
for another minute or so

01:04:20.680 --> 01:04:23.630
to see if there are any final questions,

01:04:23.630 --> 01:04:27.220
but Jordan this was absolutely fantastic.

01:04:27.220 --> 01:04:32.220
And thank you so much
for this wonderful talk

01:04:32.710 --> 01:04:36.778
and I so wish you could have
been here in person, but.

01:04:36.778 --> 01:04:37.680
- I'll come another time.

01:04:37.680 --> 01:04:39.180
I love that you have a
center for this, by the way.

01:04:39.180 --> 01:04:41.110
I wish we had a center for the
science and the humanities,

01:04:41.110 --> 01:04:43.313
like what a wonderful concept.

01:04:44.350 --> 01:04:46.550
- Yeah, and we would love to invite you

01:04:46.550 --> 01:04:49.450
and have you come visit in person.

01:04:49.450 --> 01:04:54.450
So let's all thank Jordan
for an excellent talk

01:04:54.480 --> 01:04:58.000
for sharing his wonderful insights,

01:04:58.000 --> 01:05:00.530
across many, many disciplines

01:05:00.530 --> 01:05:03.990
and for getting us all very excited

01:05:03.990 --> 01:05:07.460
about new ways to think about mathematics.

01:05:07.460 --> 01:05:08.293
Thank you so much.

01:05:08.293 --> 01:05:10.940
Thanks everyone for attending.

01:05:10.940 --> 01:05:14.950
I just want to close
with our upcoming events

01:05:14.950 --> 01:05:16.333
in the Frankie program.

01:05:17.170 --> 01:05:18.490
We have a book talk,

01:05:18.490 --> 01:05:21.700
